#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A small stand-in for a Jellyfin server, for a test instance of Bloom.

    dev/mock-jellyfin [--host ADDR] [--port N] [--port-file FILE]

It serves a fixed library (movies, two shows, a collection) with pictures it
draws itself, so the pages of the app load and paint with no server and no
network. It has no media files: a play fails, as it does for an item whose
file is gone. A request it does not know gets an empty answer and a line on
stderr, so a gap shows in the log. `dev/test-server` starts it for
`BLOOM_TEST_SERVER=mock`.

BLOOM_MOCK_PICTURE=rrggbb makes every picture that one colour, for a check
of what the app paints (dev/paint-check).

BLOOM_MOCK_STRICT=1 answers as Jellyfin does where the app could lean on a
lenient mock: a path it does not know gets 404 (not an empty 200), and a
request without the token gets 401, but for the endpoints that need no
sign-in. dev/ios-next turns it on.

The test's hands (`/mock/...`, no token needed): POST /mock/reset puts the
library, the settings, the playlists and the plugin's data back as they
were at the start; GET /mock/remote lists the play commands sent to other
sessions; GET /mock/counts counts the library pages and searches served;
POST /mock/search?hold=1 holds every search answer until ?hold=0;
GET /mock/accept-language gives the Accept-Language of the last request.
POST /mock/folders adds a library of folders (until the next reset).

BLOOM_MOCK_EXTRA_MOVIES=N adds N films ("The Extra Reel 1" ...) to the
Movies library, so a grid and a search have pages to scroll (the
performance tests of dev/ios-next perf). `--host 0.0.0.0` listens on every
interface, for a phone on the house network (dev/ios-next perf-device);
the default is 127.0.0.1.
"""

